What started me thinking was a game last night, when I'd (to my
surprise) managed to survive a room full of monsters (not a zoo, but
maybe escapees from one).  Was an altar in the room, so I sacrificed
the freshest kills, and was 'rewarded' by something landing near my
feet.  Picked it up. 'A katana named Snickersnee'.  Great, I thought,
I'll wield it. 'The katana welds itself to your hand'.  Found a scroll
of identify among the carnage and identified it.  'A cursed -3 katana
named Snickersnee.'  Strange way for the gods to show their pleasure, I
thought.  All went downhill from there; so what else was there to do
but think?  Anyhow, came up with 3 thoughts.

One is purely psychological -- I think I'd find things a bit less
frustrating if the gods would NOT 'seem pleased' but do nothing.  It's
the psychology I'm after, not the effect, so what I'm suggesting is
simply that that message be changed, so that if the gods are not going
to help, you hear something like 'no-one seems to be listening' or 'Athena
says you are on your own'.  What gets me is the 'Athena seems to be
pleased (but decides to ignore you while the mass of monsters around
you finish beating you to death while you are still praying)'.  Another
alternative to changing the message might be to provide a 'life-saving'
effect, perhaps a guarantee that if the gods are pleased you won't go
below 1HP left at the end of the prayer.  (I don't have any trouble
with the reverse side, 'The gods seem angry (but decide to let you off
with a warning)'.)  Another alternative might be to allow you the
option to stop praying if something happens while you are praying.
This would, of course, carry a fair risk of annoying the gods.

Second thought is simply a way of saving typing.  Would be nice if
engraving would always work the first time, IF you are not 'under
threat' and not blind when you try it.  (Should be able to write neatly
when you're not panicking, after all.)  You can get this effect anyway
by engraving and then :'ing until you see it's right, so this should
only save typing, not change the gameplay.  I'd keep the current
probabilities for error if you are engraving while under pressure.
Criteria could be something simple, like 'no monsters in the room or
within N squares'.  (The 'eating fix' may make this easier.  At present,
I don't dare eat anything without E'ing first -- even if I know there's
nothing else but me on the level when I start.  Lost too many promising
games by being battered unexpectedly while snacking.)

Third thought was a general wondering about whether cursed/blessed
items other than weapons and wearable things is a good idea.  I think
it may have taken some of the skill out of the game, and increased the
luck dependency.  Used to be that you learned what things did, and they
did them.  Now, even when you've learned to identify objects, you can't
be sure what they will do, unless you can tell their cursed/blessed
status.  (Original cursing, of weapons and wearable items, didn't
change WHAT they did, just meant you couldn't STOP them from doing
it and maybe that they wouldn't do it as well.)  Actually, cursed other
things seem to be solidly built-in, so I think what I'm suggesting is
that maybe altars could be made more common -- or portable :-)

Of course, I could always address that last problem by playing as a
Priest, but my personal rules don't allow that.  I keep a record of my
high score for each character class, and always play whichever class
I've got the lowest high score in.  (Means I'm presently stuck being a
Healer, and probably got a LONG way to go before I get to be a Valkyrie
again. :-)

